  "description": "<p>ARCH (Adaptive Reactive CUDA Hydrodynamics) is a C++20 and CUDA simulation code designed for finite-volume modeling of compressible hydrodynamics, heat transfer, and nuclear reactions with adaptive mesh refinement. Its CPU and CUDA backends share identical physical and numerical formulations. Version 1.3.0 introduces the optional ARCH Studio graphical desktop environment, providing researchers with an integrated workflow to edit simulation parameters, configure and build targets, inspect initial fields and AMR mesh layouts, execute or restart runs, and examine read-only plotfiles.</p><p>This deposit provides the complete source tree based on the tagged v1.3.0 release, accompanied by post-release citation information and deposit documentation outlined in ARCHIVE_PROVENANCE.json. The scientific source code and test configurations remain identical to the tagged release. No raw simulation outputs, prebuilt scientific binaries, or fetched Git LFS payloads are added. Git LFS pointers remain as pointers; users should obtain required external data and system dependencies as described in the build guide.</p><p>Supported execution is Linux and WSL2. Software qualification and validation remain scoped rather than universal; details regarding supported configurations, numerical tolerances, and specific validation limits are provided in the release notes. Researchers should not assume that all physical models, equation-of-state or reaction network combinations, execution devices, or scientific mechanisms have been validated. In addition, GNN functionality is outside the scope of this release.</p><p>All ARCH-authored material is licensed under MIT. Third-party components, scientific sources, and data retain their own terms and notices as detailed in LICENSE, THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, and the LICENSES/ directory.</p><p>AI-assisted tools were used for bug tracking and fixes, review of tolerance settings across the complete CTest suite, editing selected documentation, code completion, and engineering and security checks. The overall software architecture was designed and built by human contributors. Human maintainers retain responsibility for scientific definitions, numerical acceptance criteria, code review, and release decisions.</p>",
